From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Thu, 27 Aug 2026 10:14:54 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 10099/11362] Fix the exp6605/exp6607 GPU receipt load-phase
 stage; re-run both clean

The one failing authenticity criterion, `worker_pid_present all during`, was
the FIRST TWO samples of the run.

  sample 1  15:34:17.58  mem 266 MB    util 0%    pids: {harness}
  sample 2  15:34:18.77  mem 470 MB    util 7%    pids: {harness}
  sample 3  15:34:19.85  mem 21111 MB  util 100%  pids: {harness, 459483}

Both were taken inside the model-load poll loop, 0.1 s and 1.3 s after Popen.
The worker holds no CUDA context until load finishes, so it was genuinely not
on the card yet. `nvidia-smi` was right at every step and the predicate was
right to say False. This is neither a query race nor a gap in the run: the
266 -> 470 -> 21111 MB climb across those three samples IS the load.

The fix is in the sampler, not the predicate. The load loop now labels its
samples `load`. Samples taken while the server actually serves rows keep
`during` and stay under the full check. exp6607 carried the identical defect
and gets the identical fix; its own block had a different cause (it lost the
card to exp6605), so the defect was latent there.

The predicate got STRICTER in the same change, not looser. `_worker_on_card`
now requires the session's worker PID to appear in that sample's own recorded
process list, so a `worker_pid_present` flag written without matching evidence
fails closed. A receipt that relabels its serving samples `load` to dodge the
check fails the two-sample `during` floor instead. The row loop also samples on
the first row a session serves, so a resumed session still meets that floor.

Proven against the recorded evidence and then live:

  recorded session, new predicate                     False
  same session, load phase labelled correctly         True  (28 during)
  same session, worker removed from every pid list    False
  same session, flag forced True but no pid on card   False
  same session, all serving samples relabelled load   False

Mutations, in a PYTHONPATH-pinned worktree with the loaded file verified by
import: blanking the presence check, dropping the process-list confirmation,
and removing the two-sample floor each go RED in both suites; baseline restored
GREEN each time.

Live re-runs on GPU 0, both through the experiment modules:

  exp6605  complete  216 rows  readiness 1.0  stages {before 1, load 11,
           during 19, after 1}  all_sessions_authentic true
  exp6607  complete  216 rows  readiness 0.0  stages {before 1, load 9,
           during 19, after 1}  all_sessions_authentic true

exp6607's readiness 0.0 is a measured finding, not a block: held exact success
is 0.1667, below the frozen 0.20 floor.

The cascade is PARTLY unblocked. exp6608 now clears
`blocked_family_baseline_evidence` and builds `complete_frozen_eligibility`
with readiness 1.0, but its writer still refuses with
`reducer_checks_failed:row_replay`. That is a separate pre-existing defect that
only became reachable once a family produced replayable rows: 23 exp6605 rows
carry `finish_reason: length` and are correctly charged `invalid_generation`,
but the reducer's reduced row does not carry `finish_reason`, so
`_failure_from_replay` re-derives `syntax_failure` for exactly those rows. Left
unfixed here: it changes another experiment's failure taxonomy, and whether a
truncated plan is `invalid_generation` or `syntax_failure` is that owner's
call. exp6606 is still blocked at the conductor gate for a missing
`prior_failures` field, which needs a roadmap edit.

Two exp6608 tests asserted that the checked-in upstreams stay blocked, so
repairing an upstream turned them red. They now inject blocked sources instead
of reading `results/`, which is evidence and moves. One call still exercises
the on-disk path, asserting only the invariants that hold in any upstream
state. It cannot assert a clean report while the `row_replay` defect stands,
and says so rather than asserting around it. Scoped coverage stays 100%.

gitleaks refused the exp6607 artifact over its `cache_provenance.blob_key`, the
HuggingFace cache blob name, which IS that file's SHA-256 and is also stored in
the same artifact as `trusted_sha256`. Whether it trips is luck: exp6605's key
clears the entropy floor and exp6607's does not, for two artifacts written by
one code path on one day. Allowlisted by path only and for `generic-api-key`
only, per this config's own note that `regexes` scoping leaks repo-wide. Two
regression tests: the blob key is suppressed, and a real `ghp_` token planted in
the same artifact is still reported. Both mutation-proved -- removing the
allowlist and widening it past `generic-api-key` each go RED.

Two mutation-gate markers were retired deliberately. Neither named damage from
a test: one was armed by this task's own pytest run and named only a yosys log
that suite never touches, the other by an exp6678 run this task did not launch
and named this task's deliberate artifact writes. No `git checkout --` was run
over either list, and the yosys log is left unstaged for its owner. The
reasoning is recorded in ops/known-issues.md.

The prior blocked artifacts and the stale-label checkpoint are preserved under
the job scratch tree. No artifact was hand-edited.

The module and test edits themselves landed earlier in ce74763ce8, swept in by
a concurrent conductor `git add -A`.

